    {
      "id": "q5j48eh",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "FORCE"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Force is a push or pull capable of changing an object's shape, speed, direction of motion, or bringing it to rest.",
        "Newton's First Law is also known as the Law of Inertia, describing an object's tendency to resist changes in its state of motion.",
        "Newton's Second Law defines force as the rate of change of momentum, yielding the formula F = ma.",
        "Newton's Third Law states that every action has an equal and opposite reaction, as seen in walking, swimming, and gun recoil.",
        "Friction opposes motion between surfaces; static, sliding, and rolling are its main types, with rolling friction being the least."
      ],
      "id": "f5pq1zi"
    },
